Ans 1: Create a GitHub account or use an existing one

GitHub is a cloud platform where developers store and share their Git repositories.
1. Open your browser and go to github.com.
2. Click Sign up in the top right corner and follow the prompts to create an account, or log in if you already have one.



Ans 2: Create a new public repository

A remote repository acts as a cloud folder for your project code.
1. Once logged in, click the "+" icon in the top right corner of the page and select New repository.
2. Set the configuration details:
	• Repository name: web-development-practice
	• Description: Optional (e.g., "My web dev course assignments")
	• Visibility: Set this strictly to Public (so your instructor can view and grade it).
3. Do NOT check any boxes for "Add a README file", ".gitignore", or "Choose a license"—keep it completely empty.
4. Click the green Create repository button.



Ans 3 & 4: Connect the local repository to the remote repository and upload files

GitHub will load a page showing a set of commands under the heading "…or push an existing repository from the command line".
1. Open the terminal panel at the bottom of your VS Code project window.
2. Link your local project folder to your new GitHub cloud folder by running this command (replace the URL with your actual GitHub repository URL):bash
git remote add origin https://github.com

3. Rename your default local branch to main so it matches GitHub's standard naming convention:bash
git branch -M main

4. Upload your files to the cloud using the push command:bash
git push -u origin main


Ans 5: Add a new file and push the second commit

Now you will practice making a fresh update locally and syncing it with the cloud version.
1. In VS Code, create a new file named README.md inside your main folder directory.
2. Open the file and type a quick summary, like: # Web Development Practice Workspace. Save the file.
3. Open your terminal and run the staging, saving, and uploading commands sequentially:bash
git add README.md
git commit -m "Add project description inside README.md file"
git push


Ans 6: Verify the commit history on GitHub

1. Refresh your browser tab showing your repository page on GitHub.
2. You will now see all of your files successfully uploaded.
3. Click on the text that displays a clock icon along with "4 commits" (or 2+ depending on your previous project state).
4. Check that your chronological list of commit messages is perfectly visible on the cloud screen.

